{"parentUuid":"c76b28d3-8b63-43c0-9da8-63fb5da3b025","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a97ae4416eb4bd8cf","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_01A7xjYeqXsmS7VQJXvb4Y91","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_018YEjJkEjKFGg1mWhP2xrsy","name":"WebSearch","input":{"query":"MiniZinc Challenge 2025 results Chuffed CP-SAT Pumpkin OR-Tools Gecode"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2060,"cache_read_input_tokens":24980,"output_tokens":251,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2060},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":1,"output_tokens":251,"cache_read_input_tokens":24980,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2060,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2060,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"}},"requestId":"req_011CaoteffbxmhwfJHkh4xGq","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"a740a299-a7f0-4177-87d0-a4d7571b519e","timestamp":"2026-05-07T21:07:46.360Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"764a3f5b-af4e-4c5c-919d-219890f6ca79","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"263c4537-7eaf-4141-8243-2bd92971b2f2","agentId":"a97ae4416eb4bd8cf","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01GAf9YzNZuqNXDaXP8HwmHp","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"\"pumpkin-solver-py\" Python pip install constraint programming\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"GitHub - CPMpy/cpmpy: Constraint Programming and Modeling library in Python, based on numpy, with direct solver access. · GitHub\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/CPMpy/cpmpy\"},{\"title\":\"GitHub - ConSol-Lab/Pumpkin: A Lazy Clause Generation Constraint Programming solver written in Rust. · GitHub\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/consol-lab/pumpkin\"},{\"title\":\"CPMpy pumpkin interface (cpmpy.solvers.pumpkin) — CPMpy 0.9.24 documentation\",\"url\":\"https://cpmpy.readthedocs.io/en/latest/api/solvers/pumpkin.html\"},{\"title\":\"python-constraint · PyPI\",\"url\":\"https://pypi.org/project/python-constraint/\"},{\"title\":\"python-constraint - Constraint Solving Problem resolver for Python\",\"url\":\"http://labix.org/python-constraint\"},{\"title\":\"cpmpy · PyPI\",\"url\":\"https://pypi.org/project/cpmpy/\"},{\"title\":\"CPMpy: Constraint Programming and Modeling in Python — CPMpy 0.9.24 documentation\",\"url\":\"https://cpmpy.readthedocs.io/\"},{\"title\":\"cpmpy.solvers.gcs — CPMpy 0.9.24 documentation\",\"url\":\"https://cpmpy.readthedocs.io/en/pumpkin/_modules/cpmpy/solvers/gcs.html\"},{\"title\":\"GitHub - python-constraint/python-constraint: Constraint Solving Problem resolver for Python · GitHub\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/python-constraint/python-constraint\"},{\"title\":\"python-constraint - conda-forge | Anaconda.org\",\"url\":\"https://anaconda.org/conda-forge/python-constraint\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, here's what I found about pumpkin-solver-py and constraint programming:\n\n## Overview\n\nPumpkin is a combinatorial optimisation solver developed by the ConSol Lab at TU Delft, based on the lazy clause generation constraint programming paradigm. The solver is written in pure Rust and follows Rust best practices.\n\n## Python Installation\n\nTo use Pumpkin with Python, you need to install the 'pumpkin-solver' python package using: `pip install pumpkin-solver`\n\n## Integration with CPMpy\n\nPumpkin is available as one of the constraint programming solvers integrated with CPMpy, a Constraint Programming and Modeling library in Python. If you're using CPMpy, you can use Pumpkin as a backend solver after installing the pumpkin-solver package.\n\n## Features\n\nPumpkin supports integer variables and several global constraints including Cumulative, Element, arithmetic constraints (linear integer inequalities, integer division, multiplication, maximum, absolute value), and clausal constraints. A unique feature of Pumpkin is that it can produce a certificate of unsatisfiability.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"1f38442a-147a-4f71-b719-36ef364bcd09","timestamp":"2026-05-07T21:07:50.926Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"764a3f5b-af4e-4c5c-919d-219890f6ca79","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"c76b28d3-8b63-43c0-9da8-63fb5da3b025","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"263c4537-7eaf-4141-8243-2bd92971b2f2","agentId":"a97ae4416eb4bd8cf","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Fcj2nvJgPedrB3mDWxnFSK","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"Glasgow Constraint Solver cumulative no_overlap CP 2025 paper proof logging\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"GitHub - ciaranm/glasgow-constraint-solver: A constraint programming solver with support for proof logging · GitHub\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/ciaranm/glasgow-constraint-solver\"},{\"title\":\"Ciaran McCreesh\",\"url\":\"https://ciaranm.github.io/\"},{\"title\":\"Releases · ciaranm/glasgow-constraint-solver\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/ciaranm/glasgow-constraint-solver/releases\"},{\"title\":\"dblp: 31st International Conference on Principles and Practice of Constraint Programming, CP 2025, Glasgow, Scotland, August 10-15, 2025\",\"url\":\"https://dblp.org/rec/conf/cp/2025.html\"},{\"title\":\"GitHub - mmcilree/gcspy: A python interface for the Glasgow Constraint Solver, a CP solver implemented as part of research into pseudo-Boolean proof-logging and auditable constraint solving.\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/mmcilree/gcspy\"},{\"title\":\"CP 2025\",\"url\":\"https://cp2025.a4cp.org/\"},{\"title\":\"The Glasgow Subgraph Solver: Using Constraint Programming to Tackle Hard Subgraph Isomorphism Problem Variants | Springer Nature Link\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-3-030-51372-6_19\"},{\"title\":\"End-to-End Verification for Subgraph Solving\",\"url\":\"https://cakeml.org/aaai24-verified-subgraphs.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"ConstraintLLM: A Neuro-Symbolic Framework for Industrial ...\",\"url\":\"https://aclanthology.org/2025.emnlp-main.809.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"CP: Principles and Practice of Constraint Programming 2027 2026 2025 ...\",\"url\":\"http://www.wikicfp.com/cfp/program?id=572\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, here's what I found about the Glasgow Constraint Solver:\n\nThe Glasgow Constraint Solver is a new project that brings proof logging to constraint programming. Encoding high level constraints in OPB format is mostly almost straightforward, because the developers deliberately use a very simple encoding, and derive any additional constraints or inference in a way that is proof-checked.\n\nThe search results show that for each constraint, the solver tests that it is defined correctly over a range of hand-crafted and randomly generated sequences of variables by enumerating all solutions to the problem that has just that constraint, and comparing them to a computer-generated list of all solutions.\n\nHowever, I was unable to find a specific CP 2025 paper specifically discussing cumulative and no_overlap constraints with proof logging from this search. The results indicate that CP 2025 is the 31st version of the CP series organized by the Association for Constraint Programming, located in Glasgow, Scotland, at the University of Glasgow. But the specific paper you're looking for may not yet be indexed in these search results, or you may need to check the CP 2025 conference proceedings directly.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"9702f19c-752f-4654-a59e-aac15721ed75","timestamp":"2026-05-07T21:07:52.121Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"c76b28d3-8b63-43c0-9da8-63fb5da3b025","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"a740a299-a7f0-4177-87d0-a4d7571b519e","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"263c4537-7eaf-4141-8243-2bd92971b2f2","agentId":"a97ae4416eb4bd8cf","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_018YEjJkEjKFGg1mWhP2xrsy","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"MiniZinc Challenge 2025 results Chuffed CP-SAT Pumpkin OR-Tools Gecode\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"MiniZinc - Challenge 2025 Results\",\"url\":\"https://www.minizinc.org/challenge/2025/results/\"},{\"title\":\"MiniZinc - Challenge 2025\",\"url\":\"https://www.minizinc.org/challenge/2025/\"},{\"title\":\"Home | MiniZinc Challenge 2025\",\"url\":\"https://challenge.minizinc.org/\"},{\"title\":\"Constraint Modeling Challenge |\",\"url\":\"https://dmcommunity.org/2025/08/19/constraint-modeling-challenge/\"},{\"title\":\"On Benchmarking MiniZinc and the LinkedIn Queens Problem - zayenz.se\",\"url\":\"https://zayenz.se/blog/post/benchmarking-linkedin-queens/\"},{\"title\":\"GitHub - MiniZinc/mzn-challenge: An archive repository containing the MiniZinc instances used for the MiniZinc Challenge\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/MiniZinc/mzn-challenge\"},{\"title\":\"Call for Problem Submission for the MiniZinc Challenge 2025\",\"url\":\"https://www.minizinc.org/challenge/2025/call_for_problems/\"},{\"title\":\"MiniZinc - Challenge\",\"url\":\"https://www.minizinc.org/challenge/\"},{\"title\":\"Philosophy of the MiniZinc challenge\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/220202299_Philosophy_of_the_MiniZinc_challenge\"},{\"title\":\"Hakan Kjellerstrand - MiniZinc Challenge 2025 Results\",\"url\":\"https://www.linkedin.com/posts/hakank_minizinc-challenge-2025-results-activity-7366861519315156992-vli1\"}]\n\nBased on my search results, I found information about the MiniZinc Challenge 2025 results:\n\nGoogle OR-Tools won all the medals in the 2025 challenge. The official results are available on the MiniZinc Challenge website.\n\nThe challenge organisers entered Chuffed and Gecode as reference solvers. Chuffed is a C++ FD solver using lazy clause generation, and Gecode is a C++ FD solver.\n\nRegarding the other solvers you mentioned:\n- Solvers tested include Gecode and other versions bundled with MiniZinc 2.9.3, with Pumpkin from the main branch\n- The search results mention CP-SAT as part of Google OR-Tools' offering, which won the challenge\n\nThe entries by challenge organisers are not eligible for prizes, but do modify the scoring results.\n\nFor detailed and comprehensive results including scores for all solvers mentioned, you can visit the official MiniZinc Challenge 2025 results page at www.minizinc.org/challenge/2025/results/\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"a4bc05a3-96b7-400a-bd3a-d25c2f778831","timestamp":"2026-05-07T21:07:52.283Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"a740a299-a7f0-4177-87d0-a4d7571b519e","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
